{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UqdPE9BcBy8ZgXAfBV58md","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what needs to be built next.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. They derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. Components include:\n- cmd/ - Go implementations of each utility\n- pkg/testutils/ - Differential testing harness\n- pkg/sys/ - Syscall abstractions\n- pkg/format/ - Output formatting\n\n\n- magefiles/ - Build targets\n\n**Current State**: \n- All PRDs are written (prd001 through prd010)\n- All use cases are written\n- All test suites are written\n- No Go code has been implemented yet\n- Status reflects spec phase, not code\n\n**Roadmap**:\n- rel01.0 (in_progress): ts specification and differential test - uc001-ts (in_progress)\n- rel01.1 (pending): wc, cat, sponge\n- rel02.0 (pending): ls core, du\n- rel02.1 (pending): ls extended\n\n**Focus**: rel01.0 is the immediate priority.\n\nFor rel01.0, I need to build two things in dependency order: the differential testing harness in pkg/testutils first, then the ts utility in cmd/ts. Since I can only create one task, I should start with pkg/testutils—it's the foundational piece that cmd/ts will depend on. This follows the principle of building test infrastructure before features.\n\nLooking at the requirements for pkg/testutils, I need to implement test case data structures, binary execution, comparison and reporting, normalization hooks, and file-state comparison. The package should be around 250-350 lines across 5-7 files, with the main components being the DiffTest struct and runner logic, normalizer implementations for handling timestamps and other variations, and file comparison utilities. I'm mapping out the file structure for the testutils package—DiffTest struct and normalization logic in difftest.go, the RunDiffTests function with binary execution and comparison in runner.go, timestamp normalizers in normalizers.go, and file-state comparison in filecompare.go, plus tests to cover everything. I'm settling on the primary exported type being DiffTest, so that goes in difftest.go along with the Normalize function type and the main RunDiffTests API. The runner.go file handles the internal execution logic and comparison mechanics, while normalizers.go contains the TimestampNormalizer and any composition utilities. I'm still working through the exact file boundaries, but this structure feels natural for the package. I'm identifying what files the stitch agent needs to read to understand the context—the PRD for testutils requirements, the go.mod for the module path, and any engineering guidelines that apply. For design decisions, I should only derive them from what's actually in the PRD, like using DiffTest as the primary exported type in difftest.go, setting the timeout default to 10 seconds based on R2.3, and using t.Run for subtests per R3.6.\n\nNow I'm composing the YAML output directly without exploring the filesystem, since the only tool I should use is Write to save the file.\n\nI'm structuring the task with the DiffTest struct and related components—defining the required reading materials, then laying out the four files I need to create: difftest.go for the core struct and entry point, runner.go for binary execution and output comparison, normalizers.go for the timestamp normalization logic, and filestate.go for file-based output validation.\n\nNow I'm adding the test file to validate the runner and normalizer implementations, then reconsidering which files are essential for the stitch agent to read—the PRD is critical for the specification, go.mod for the module path, and the architecture document for component context.\n\nLooking at the file structure more carefully, I should follow the naming convention where the primary exported type determines the filename, so DiffTest belongs in difftest.go. For a package this size, I'm thinking about whether RunDiffTests should live in the same file or be separated, and how to organize the public API across the files.\n\nI'm settling on a simpler structure: difftest.go handles the core DiffTest struct, Normalize type, and the RunDiffTests entry point along with the execution and comparison logic; normalizers.go contains the timestamp normalization and composition utilities; and filestate.go provides the file-content comparison helpers. That gives me three production files plus tests, which stays within the constraint of touching no more than 5-7 files total. I'm weighing whether to keep DiffTest and RunDiffTests together in the same file since they're closely related, and estimating the line counts: the struct and type definitions around 30 lines, the main function with execution and comparison logic around 150 lines, normalizers around 50 lines, file comparison around 40 lines, and tests around 150 lines—totaling roughly 270 lines of production code, which fits the 250-350 target. So I'll consolidate DiffTest and RunDiffTests into difftest.go, then split out the normalizers and file-state logic into separate files.\n\nNow I'm mapping the PRD requirements to task requirements, starting with R1 covering the DiffTest struct and R2 for binary execution.\n\nI need to identify the design decisions that actually come from the PRD rather than inventing them—things like the primary exported type being DiffTest and the file naming convention following the project's standards.\n\nFor the timeout handling, I'm using exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout to enforce the 10-second default specified in the requirements. Each test runs as a subtest so failures are clearly identified in the test output. The TimestampNormalizer will swap out common time format patterns for a consistent placeholder.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the acceptance criteria—the function needs to work when called from standard test functions, silent on success but verbose on failure, and the normalizer should handle timestamp replacement correctly.\n\nI'm thinking through the YAML structure now and need to reference the actual files that exist in the project: the product requirements document, architecture guide, and go.mod for the module path context.\n\nThe Go module is `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils`, so the testutils package import path would be `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils`. I'm ready to write out the final YAML task definition.\n\nFor the required_reading section, I should point the stitch agent to the PRD, architecture documentation, and go.mod so it has the full context it needs to generate the code properly. I'm settling on the final structure for the first deliverable: a testutils package with the DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests harness, pulling requirements from the PRD and architecture docs, and creating the initial difftest.go file.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the remaining files—a runner module to execute binaries and compare outputs, normalizers for handling timestamps and composing multiple normalization strategies, file-state comparison for utilities that write to disk, and comprehensive tests covering all the core functionality.\n\nI need to handle timeout behavior and flesh out the detailed requirements for the DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests function, including how to execute both the reference and target binaries, capture their outputs, apply normalizations, and report divergences. I need to implement normalization hooks that provide a TimestampNormalizer to replace common timestamp patterns with a fixed placeholder, plus a ComposeNormalizers function to chain multiple normalizers together, with no normalization applied when the slice is empty. Then I'm moving into file-state comparison logic that checks ExpectedFiles after both binaries execute. I need to wrap up the test structure by running each case individually with t.Run so failures are clearly identified in verbose output. The TimestampNormalizer needs regex patterns to handle common timestamp formats like \"Mon DD HH:MM:SS\", ISO-8601, and elapsed time, replacing all matches with a placeholder string. Finally, the acceptance criteria shows that a test package should be able to define a slice of DiffTest cases and call RunDiffTests with just the test, binary paths, and test slice—no additional setup required. A passing test run produces no output, while a failing test prints the triggering args, stdin, and both binaries' stdout, stderr, and exit code. TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps with a fixed \"TIMESTAMP\" placeholder. DiffTest correctly fails when the Go binary writes different file content than the reference binary, and tests that exceed the 10-second timeout fail with a clear message rather than hanging. All pkg/testutils code needs to compile cleanly under golangci-lint, and the test suite should pass without failures.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the test structure—I have 5 files which fits the expected range, but I need to consider whether to split tests across multiple files or keep them consolidated. For a small package, one test file works fine, though I should think about how to test RunDiffTests since it requires actual binaries to run against. how the tests would work:\n\n1. For RunDiffTests: we need two binaries that produce known output. We could use simple Go test helper binaries compiled at test time, or use standard Unix tools like `echo` or `cat`.\n2. For TimestampNormalizer: straightforward unit test with known input/output.\n3. For file-state comparison: create temp files and verify comparison.\n\nThe stitch agent should figure this out from the PRD and requirements. I don't need to over-specify the test strategy.\n\nOne more thing: I should make sure I'm not referencing files that don't exist in required_reading. Let me check:\n- docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml - YES, this exists per the project_context\n- docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml - YES, this exists\n- go.mod - YES, this exists\n\nGood. Now let me estimate the code size more carefully to stay within the 250-350 line constraint. difftest.go should be around 30 lines with the struct and type definitions, runner.go needs about 160 lines for the main function and helpers like binary execution, output comparison, and failure formatting, normalizers.go around 50 lines for the timestamp logic and composition, and filestate.go about 30 lines for file comparison. That puts me at roughly 270 lines total, which fits the requirement.\n\nFor the test file, I'm looking at around 150 lines covering tests for the main diff functionality, normalization behavior, file comparison, and timeout handling. Now I'm ready to write out the final YAML specification.\n\nLet me verify the YAML syntax one more time -- the description field needs to use a literal block scalar with the pipe character, all strings containing colons must be quoted, and the requirements, design decisions, and acceptance criteria should follow the mapping format with id and text fields. I'll also make sure to avoid Unicode em dashes and use \" -- \" instead.\n\nThe Write tool should handle creating the .cobbler directory if it doesn't exist, so I'll proceed with writing the file directly without needing a separate directory creation step. I'm finalizing the task description now, making sure it gives the stitch agent everything needed to build the testutils package independently.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":77004,"cache_read_input_tokens":14003,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tok